{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"cutprice-untameable-stork","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Summary\n\n`fsck` is a DIAGNOSTIC tool: finding problems is its purpose and its success condition. The MCP\ntool nevertheless converts a completed report into `command_failed`, so the caller receives an\nerror object with the entire report stuffed into a message STRING rather than a structured\nresult.\n\n## Mechanism\n\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py:396` — the tool calls `rebar.fsck(recover=..., report_only=...)`.\n- `src/rebar/_lib_reads.py` — the library raises `RebarError(f\"rebar fsck failed (exit {_rc}): ...\")`\n  whenever the underlying exit code is non-zero (documented as the 'prior _ok contract').\n- `src/rebar/_commands/fsck.py:236-238` — the CLI exits non-zero when `issue_count` > 0.\n\n## What is EXPECTED and what is the BUG\n\n**Expected: the CLI exiting non-zero on findings.** That is the conventional contract for an\nintegrity checker (`git fsck`, `fsck(8)`) and scripts depend on it. Not a defect.\n\n**The bug is the MCP/library layer treating that as a FAILED CALL.** The run succeeded; it\nproduced precisely the diagnosis requested. Consequences for a caller:\n- the result is not machine-readable — findings arrive as one newline-joined string inside an\n  error field, so a client cannot enumerate them;\n- an agent reasonably concludes the TOOL is broken and stops, when the tool worked and the STORE\n  has findings — two very different situations that must not share a signal;\n- it is inconsistent with the surrounding read surface, which returns structured results.\n\n## Observed (parallel Copilot session, live server)\n\n12 findings: 7 `STATUS_FORK_RESOLVED`, 4 `ORPHAN_EVENT`, 1 `ENV_ID_MISMATCH`, all delivered as\n`{\"error\": \"command_failed\", \"exit_code\": 1, \"message\": \"...\"}`.\n\n## Secondary: 'resolved' findings should probably not fail the run\n\n7 of the 12 are `STATUS_FORK_RESOLVED ... concurrent claim/status race resolved (dropped uuid=...)`.\nThose describe races fsck SELF-HEALED. Counting a self-healed condition toward a failure exit\nconflates 'I fixed something' with 'something is wrong', and on a busy multi-agent store those\nwill recur indefinitely, so the tool is permanently 'failing' by design.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The MCP `fsck` tool returns a STRUCTURED result (findings enumerable by kind and ticket)\n      for a completed run, whatever the findings.\n- [ ] A genuine execution failure (fsck could not run) remains distinguishable from a completed\n      run that found issues.\n- [ ] The CLI's non-zero-on-findings exit is PRESERVED — scripts depend on it; only the\n      library/MCP surfacing changes.\n- [ ] Decide, and record, whether self-resolved `STATUS_FORK_RESOLVED` entries count toward the\n      failure exit; if they do not, the CLI's exit reflects that.\n\n## Reported by\n\nA parallel Copilot CLI session against the deployed server.","id":"326f-595a-088b-40a5","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","fsck","dx","api"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"MCP fsck turns a SUCCESSFUL diagnostic report into a tool error, so the report is unusable"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787858225689088001,"uuid":"3645621b-c3ad-49e6-9cb6-73f441fe63a9"}